    {'heading': 'Derivative or Secondary Conjugations.'},
    {'para': '**497.** Secondary conjugations are those in which a whole system of forms, more or less complete, is made from a derivative conjugation-stem, this whole system being usually connected with a certain definite modification of the original radical sense. These conjugations are: I. Passive. II. Intensive. III. Desiderative. IV. Causative. V. Denominative.'},
    {'para': '**498. I. Passive.** The present-system of the passive has been described; as also the peculiar 3rd pers. sing. used as aor. pass., the past pass. participle in ⟨ta⟩ or ⟨na⟩, and the fut. pass. participles or gerundives. In all other parts of the verb middle forms are used, if necessary, with passive meaning.'},
    {'para': '**499. II. Intensive.** The intensive conjugation signifies the intensification or the repetition of the action expressed by the primary conjugation of a root. Forms outside the present-system are too rare to need notice here; indeed, even within that system they are by no means common in the later language. Intensives fall into two classes.'},
    {'para': '**500.** 1. The verbs of the first class (only act.) form their intensive-stem by reduplication, and the reduplicating syllable is strengthened. *a.* Radical ⟨a⟩ and ⟨ṛ⟩ are reduplicated with ⟨ā⟩, ⟨i⟩ and ⟨ī⟩ with ⟨e⟩, ⟨u⟩ and ⟨ū⟩ with ⟨o⟩; thus, ⟨vāvad⟩, ⟨dādhṛ⟩, ⟨nenī⟩, ⟨joghuṣ⟩. *b.* Sometimes the reduplicating syllable has a final consonant, taken from the end of the root; thus, ⟨carcar⟩, ⟨marmṛj⟩. *c.* Sometimes the reduplication is dissyllabic, an *i*-vowel being inserted after the final consonant of the reduplicating syllable; thus, ⟨varīvṛt⟩. — The model of inflection is the present-system of the reduplicating-class, but deviations are not rare; in particular, an ⟨i⟩ is sometimes inserted between stem and ending.'},
    {'para': '**501.** 2. From the intensive-stem as just described may be formed another, formally identical with a passive-stem, by the suffix ⟨ya⟩. It takes middle inflection, but has no passive value, being used precisely as is the intensive just mentioned. Thus, ⟨mṛj⟩, ⟨marmṛjyate⟩.'},
    {'para': '**502.** A few intensives, having lost their value as such, come to be used as presents, and are treated by the native grammarians as simple roots. Thus ⟨jāgṛ⟩, really intensive of ⟨gṛ⟩ ‘wake’, is assigned to the root-class: pres. ⟨jāgarmi⟩ etc., du. ⟨jāgṛvas⟩ etc.; impf. 1. ⟨ajāgaram⟩, 2. ⟨ajāgar⟩, 3. ⟨ajāgar⟩; du. ⟨ajāgṛva⟩ etc. So'},
    {'para': 'also ⟨daridrā⟩, intens. of ⟨drā⟩ ‘run’, used as a present with the sense ‘be poor’. ⟨nij⟩ ‘wash’, and some others, use the intensive present-system in the same way, and are assigned to the reduplicating class; thus, 3rd sing. ⟨nenekti⟩, 3rd pl. ⟨nenijati⟩.'},
    {'para': 'Intensive forms outside the present-system are very rare.'},
    {'para': '**503. III. Desiderative.** By this conjugation is denoted a desire for the action or condition denoted by the simple root; thus, ⟨pibāmi⟩ ‘I drink’, desid. ⟨pipāsāmi⟩ ‘I wish to drink’.'},
    {'para': '**504.** To form the desiderative-stem the root is reduplicated, and adds ⟨sa⟩, sometimes ⟨iṣa⟩. The consonant of the reduplication is determined by the usual rules; the vowel of reduplication is ⟨i⟩ if the root has an *a*-vowel, an *i*-vowel, or ⟨ṛ⟩, and ⟨u⟩ if the root has an *u*-vowel. Thus, ⟨yā⟩, ⟨yiyāsati⟩; ⟨nī⟩, ⟨ninīṣati⟩; ⟨bhū⟩, ⟨bubhūṣati⟩; ⟨kṛ⟩, ⟨cikīrṣati⟩; ⟨bhid⟩, ⟨bibhitsati⟩; ⟨tij⟩, ⟨titikṣate⟩.'},
    {'para': '**505.** A number of roots form an abbreviated desiderative-stem; thus, ⟨āp⟩, ⟨īpsati⟩; ⟨dā⟩, ⟨ditsati⟩.'},
    {'para': '**506.** The conjugation in the present-system is like that of other *a*-stems. Outside of that desiderative forms are quite infrequent. The perfect is the periphrastic. The aorist is of the *iṣ*-form; thus, ⟨īpsa⟩, ⟨aipsiṣat⟩; ⟨atitikṣiṣṭa⟩. The futures are made with the auxiliary vowel ⟨i⟩; thus, ⟨īpsiṣyati⟩, ⟨īpsitāsmi⟩. The verbal nouns are made with ⟨i⟩ in all forms where that vowel is ever taken. A passive may be made; thus, ⟨īpsyate⟩ ‘it is desired to be obtained’; part. ⟨īpsita⟩.'},
    {'para': '**507. IV. Causative.** 1. The present-system of the causative has been treated of already. 2. The perfect is the periphrastic, the derivative noun in ⟨ā⟩ being formed from the causative-stem; thus, ⟨dhārayāṃ cakāra⟩. 3. The aorist is the reduplicated, made in general directly from the root, and formally unconnected with the causative-stem; thus, ⟨dhṛ⟩, ⟨adīdharam⟩, ⟨abūbhuvam⟩. In a few instances, where the root has assumed a peculiar form before the causative-sign, the reduplicated aorist is made from this form, not from the'},
    {'para': 'simple root; thus, ⟨sthā⟩, ⟨sthāpayati⟩, ⟨atiṣṭhipat⟩. 4. Both futures are made from the causative-stem, the auxiliary ⟨i⟩ replacing the final ⟨a⟩; thus, ⟨dhārayiṣyati⟩, ⟨dhārayitāsmi⟩. 5. The verbal nouns and adjectives are in part formed from the causative-stem in the same manner as the futures, in part from the causatively strengthened root-form; thus, pass. part. ⟨śrāvita⟩; fut. pass. part. (gerundive) ⟨tarpayitavya⟩, ⟨sthāpya⟩; inf. ⟨joṣayitum⟩; gerund ⟨sādayitvā⟩, ⟨sthāpya⟩, ⟨gamayya⟩ (§ 310).'},
    {'para': '**508. Causative passive and desiderative.** These may be made from the causative-stem as follows. 1. The passive-stem is formed by adding the usual passive sign ⟨ya⟩ to the causatively strengthened root, the syllables ⟨aya⟩ being omitted; thus, ⟨dhāryate⟩. 2. The desiderative-stem is made by reduplication and addition of the syllables ⟨iṣa⟩, of which the ⟨i⟩ replaces the final ⟨a⟩ of the causative-stem; thus, ⟨didhārayiṣati⟩, ⟨bibhāvayiṣati⟩. This is a rare formation.'},
    {'para': '**509. V. Denominative.** A denominative conjugation is one that has as basis a noun-stem. In general, the base is made from the noun-stem by means of the conjugation-sign ⟨ya⟩, which has the accent. Intermediate between the denominative and causative conjugations stands a class of verbs plainly denominative in origin but having the causative accent. Thus, from ⟨mantra⟩, ⟨mantrayate⟩ *mantráyate*; from ⟨kīrti⟩, ⟨kīrtayati⟩ *kīrtáyati*. See § 76.'},
    {'para': '**510.** The denominative meaning is of the greatest variety; e. g. ‘be like’, ‘act as’, ‘regard or treat as’, ‘make into’, ‘desire, crave’ — that which is signified by the noun-stem. Examples: from ⟨tapas⟩ ‘penance, asceticism’, ⟨tapasyati⟩ ‘practise ascetism’; from ⟨namas⟩, ⟨namasyati⟩ ‘honor’; ⟨kṛṣṇāyate⟩ ‘blacken’; ⟨aśvāyati⟩ ‘seek horses’; ⟨gopāyati⟩ ‘play the herdsman, protect’; ⟨vasūyati⟩ ‘desire wealth’; ⟨bhiṣajyati⟩ ‘play the physician, cure’; ⟨putrakāmyati⟩ ‘desire a son’, from the poss. cpd ⟨putrakāma⟩ ‘desiring a son’.'},
